  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Mohan Lal vs State on 18 July, 2017",
    "facts": "2. Appeal filed by the petitioner was dismissed in default on February 7, 1989. 3. The police authorities could not locate the petitioner who was on bail before the Court of Sessions till September, 2002. Unaware of the appeal being dismissed the petitioner sought bail from the Court of Sessions which dismissed the application seeking bail on September 12, 2002. The petitioner was in Court (2 of 3) [CRLR-1014/2002] and was taken into custody. He was granted bail by this Court in the instant revision petition on January 6, 2003 and was able to furnish the bail bonds and surety bonds 4 days later. In this manner the petitioner has been in jail for four months. 4. Two options are available. The first is to remand the matter to the learned District Judge who dismissed the appeal in default for the reason that for fault of Counsel the petitioner should not suffer. Sent to the public analyst report came that the milk sample lifted was adulterated on account of the fat and solids not being fat, being less than the prescribed standard. Mohan Lal vs State on 18 July, 2017 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/27335328/ 1 6.",
    "arguments": "Learned counsel for the petitioner has not been able to point out any infirmity in the lifting of the sample, its analysis or the authority of the Inspector who lifted the sample.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "Learned counsel for the petitioner has not been able to point out any infirmity in the lifting of the sample, its analysis or the authority of the Inspector who lifted the sample.",
